------------------------------------- State management ------------------------------------------------

- DISPLAY OUR LOGGED IN USER AND ADD A LOGOUT FEATURE WITH THE HELP OF SIGNALS.

- Instructions:

Now that we can log in, it would be nice to display the logged-in user somewhere.
To achieve that, we will use a common pattern: a service with a signal that components can access.
The idea is to expose a read-only signal in UserService. The signal will initially be undefined.
When the user logs in, the signal will be set with the current user. All the interested components
will access this signal to do whatever they need to do.

1️⃣ Let’s start by setting up this signal in our UserService. Create a private field called user,
    which will be initialized with a signal containing undefined (the signal value should be typed
    as UserModel | undefined). Then add a public field called currentUser to expose the private
    field as a read-only signal (you can mark this field as readonly as well).

        private readonly user = signal<UserModel | undefined>(undefined);
        readonly currentUser = this.user.asReadonly();


2️⃣ We now want to set the user in case of authentication success. To do that, 🔥you can use the RxJS
    tap operator in the authenticate method, which allows executing an action without transforming
    the event as the map operator does. In the tap operator, set the user signal with the received
    user.


3️⃣ In the component Menu, inject the UserService and store the currentUser signal in a component’s
    field called user.
    We can now display the current user in the template using this user signal. In the navbar,
    below the ul containing the Races link, add a new ul, which displays the user, its score, and
    a logout button:
    menu.html:

        <ul class="navbar-nav">
            <li class="navbar-text">
                <span id="current-user" class="me-2">
                {{ user.login }}
                <span class="fa fa-star"></span>
                {{ user.money | number }}
                </span>
            </li>
            <li class="nav-item">
                <button id="logout-link" type="button" class="btn nav-link" aria-label="Logout">
                <span class="fa fa-power-off"></span>
                </button>
            </li>
        </ul>
    
    Of course, this 👉ul element should only be displayed if the user is logged in, i.e. when user
    has a value. 👉The "Races" link should also be displayed only if the user is logged in.

    The number pipe allows to format the score with thousands separators (and so you have to import
    the DecimalPipe from @angular/common).


4️⃣ Let’s implement the logout feature now. When the user clicks on the button, call a method in
    the component. This method should call the logout method of the UserService which must reset
    the user signal to undefined. The method of the component should also redirect the user to the
    home page thanks to router.navigateByUrl().


5️⃣ We are now going to do almost the same thing in Home. 👉We indeed want the Register and Login
    buttons to appear only if the user is not logged in, and 👉the Races button to appear only if the
    user is logged in. To do so, get the current user signal in a new field as we did in Menu.
    Then, display the buttons conditionally.


